Accept Android in the user-data-path platform switch.

doGetUserDataPath switches on process.platform directly rather than going through
isLinux, so patch 0001 does not reach it, and 'android' falls to the default:
throw new Error('Platform not supported').

The main server never hits it: we pass --user-data-dir, and the CLI value returns
early well before the switch. The Pty Host is forked without that argument, so it
does hit it, and the throw kills the process during construction:

  [IPC Library: Pty Host] Unhandled Promise Rejection: Error: Platform not supported
      at uc (.../out/vs/platform/terminal/node/ptyHostMain.js:31:26877)

The workbench then reports "Channel name 'ptyHost' timed out after 1000ms" and no
terminal can start. Found by booting the Code - OSS tree on an API 36 emulator;
planning had this line recorded as unreachable, which is true only of the process
that receives the CLI argument.

Falling through to the linux case is right rather than merely convenient: the
value is XDG_CONFIG_HOME or ~/.config, and both are correct here. It is also
moot in practice, since every process that reaches this code on device is given
an explicit --user-data-dir; what matters is that the default arm no longer
throws.

diff --git a/src/vs/platform/environment/node/userDataPath.ts b/src/vs/platform/environment/node/userDataPath.ts
index 98b4411..0c8988e 100644
--- a/src/vs/platform/environment/node/userDataPath.ts
+++ b/src/vs/platform/environment/node/userDataPath.ts
@@ -96,6 +96,7 @@ export function getDefaultUserDataPath(productName: string): string {
 			appDataPath = join(homedir(), 'Library', 'Application Support');
 			break;
 		case 'linux':
+		case 'android':
 			appDataPath = process.env['XDG_CONFIG_HOME'] || join(homedir(), '.config');
 			break;
 		default:
